   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public officers law, in relation to the accessibility of\npublic hearings and meetings",
    "basePrintNo": "S3430",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3430-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 12,
       "fullName": "Michael Gianaris",
       "imgName": "383_michael_gianaris.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 383,
       "sessionMemberId": 1808,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GIANARIS"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§74-a & 103, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Public Officers Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3430\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public officers law, in relation to the accessibil-\nity of public hearings and meetings\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide interpreters and assisted listening devices for deaf or hard\nof hearing impaired individuals at public hearings and meetings.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nAmends section 74-a of the Public Officers Law requiring the services of\ninterpreters for the deaf or hard of hearing be made available upon\nrequest of a deaf or hard of hearing individual at public hearings, as\nwell as requiring certain hearing rooms to be equipped with assisted\nlistening devices. Amends section 103 of the Public Officers Law adding\nnew subdivisions to ensure the provision of interpreters at meetings\nconducted by public bodies, as well as requiring certain hearing rooms\nto be equipped with assisted listening devices.\n\nProvisions are included to exempt the public bodies from the requirement\nto provide the services of an interpreter or assisted listening devices\nif such actions pose an undue hardship.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe services of interpreters, and assisted listening devices, for indi-\nviduals who are deaf or hard of hearing are essential if such persons\nare to fully participate in public hearings and meetings conducted by\ngovernmental bodies. Similar provisions already exist for administrative\nrule making proceedings. Individuals who are deaf or hard of hearing who\nwish to attend and participate at such public hearings and meetings such\nshould be afforded every opportunity possible to fully engage consistent\nwith their constitutional rights of free speech and equal protection.\n\nAdditionally, this bill is reasonably drafted and does not require a\nhard mandate on local governments; to wit, at least 5 exemptions exist\nfor localities if such services:\n\n1. Represent an undue hardship\n\n2. Such services are not requested within a reasonable time\n\n3. Such services are not requested in writing\n\n4. Such services are not available S. The meeting room holds less than\n100 persons\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  2017-18: S.8129 - Comrie/A.8158 7 pick-\nens-Passed Assembly.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nMinor costs associated with the hiring of interpreters on a per meeting\nbasis, and with equipping hearing rooms with assisted listening devices.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
